From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kv-cache <noreply@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Sep 2001 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vllm: do not assert on a late KV transfer completion

_update_from_kv_xfer_finished() asserts that every req_id handed back by a
finished transfer is still in self.requests. It is driven by ASYNCHRONOUS
completions, so that is not an invariant the scheduler can hold: a completion
can always land after the request has been finished or aborted, and it
reliably does so when the transport failed and the request was torn down with
bytes still in flight.

The consequence is out of all proportion to the fault. A per-request,
recoverable transport error becomes an AssertionError inside the engine busy
loop, which raises EngineDeadError and takes the entire server down. On a
disaggregated pair it takes the peer with it: when our decode died this way the
prefill followed with RuntimeError: cancelled, so a single timed-out transfer
cost both halves and required a full teardown of both hosts to recover.

Reproduced on a two-node NIXL/UCX P/D pair, Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct TP=4 per side,
KV moving over RoCE:

  ucx_utils.cpp:204  UCX AM send failed with status -80 (Endpoint timeout)
  scheduler.py:2648  assert req_id in self.requests
  AssertionError -> EngineDeadError -> EngineCore gone

Three times, with two different triggers -- one deep request (4096-token
context, concurrency 1) and a batch of shallow ones (1024-token context,
concurrency 8) -- so it is not a concurrency race. The common factor is
transfer volume: enough bytes in flight to reach the endpoint timeout.

Both loops are guarded, not just the one in the traceback. finished_sending is
what fired for us, but finished_recving has the identical assert on the
identical asynchronous path and differs only in which side of the pair is
holding the request.

Dropping the unknown id is safe rather than merely survivable. self.requests is
the scheduler's own index; a request leaves it via finish or abort, which is
the same transition that frees its blocks. So if the id is gone the blocks are
already back, _free_blocks() would be a double free, and adding it to
finished_recving_kv_req_ids would schedule a dead request. Skipping with a
warning is the only correct action, and the warning is what makes the
underlying transport fault visible instead of silent.

This does NOT fix the UCX timeout, which is a separate defect and the actual
fault. It stops that fault from being fatal.

diff --git a/vllm/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py b/vllm/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py
--- a/vllm/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py
+++ b/vllm/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py
@@ -2628,6 +2628,26 @@
         * if finished_sending: free the blocks
         # if finished_recving: add to state so we can
             schedule the request during the next step.
+
+        A req_id arriving here that is NOT in self.requests is tolerated and
+        dropped. This path is driven by ASYNCHRONOUS transfer completions, so
+        a completion can always land after the scheduler has already finished
+        or aborted the request -- most obviously when the transport failed and
+        the request was torn down while bytes were still in flight. Asserting
+        here turns a recoverable, per-request transport fault into a fatal
+        EngineCore crash that takes the whole server with it, and on a
+        disaggregated pair it takes the peer down too.
+
+        Observed on NIXL/UCX P/D: `UCX AM send failed with status -80
+        (Endpoint timeout)` immediately followed by AssertionError here, on
+        both a single deep request and a batch of shallow ones. See the repo
+        handoff for the reproduction.
+
+        Dropping is safe, not merely survivable: self.requests is the scheduler's
+        own index, and a request leaves it via finish/abort, which is also what
+        frees its blocks. If the id is gone the blocks are already back, so
+        _free_blocks would be a double free and rescheduling would be a use of
+        a dead request.
         """
 
         if self.connector is not None:
@@ -2636,7 +2656,15 @@
         # KV Connector:: update recv and send status from last step.
         for req_id in kv_connector_output.finished_recving or ():
             logger.debug("Finished recving KV transfer for request %s", req_id)
-            assert req_id in self.requests
+            if req_id not in self.requests:
+                # Late completion for a request the scheduler has already let
+                # go. Not an error: see the note above _update_from_kv_xfer_
+                # finished. Its blocks went back with the request, so there is
+                # nothing to free and nothing to schedule.
+                logger.warning(
+                    "Discarding finished_recving for unknown request %s "
+                    "(already finished or aborted).", req_id)
+                continue
             req = self.requests[req_id]
             if req.status == RequestStatus.WAITING_FOR_REMOTE_KVS:
                 self.finished_recving_kv_req_ids.add(req_id)
@@ -2645,7 +2673,11 @@
                 self._free_blocks(self.requests[req_id])
         for req_id in kv_connector_output.finished_sending or ():
             logger.debug("Finished sending KV transfer for request %s", req_id)
-            assert req_id in self.requests
+            if req_id not in self.requests:
+                logger.warning(
+                    "Discarding finished_sending for unknown request %s "
+                    "(already finished or aborted).", req_id)
+                continue
             self._free_blocks(self.requests[req_id])
 
     def _update_requests_with_invalid_blocks(
